 C. Scharl\n\nOnline: In Stock\n\n$27.00\n\n9798385232895\n\nJanuary 2025\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMore nuclear symbolism | Restricted Data (https://blog.nuclearsecrecy.com/2013/01/22/more-nuclear-symbolism/)\nciteturn546search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; \nThe simbolism of these deviced cannot be explained because AEC classified them as “SECRET”. \n\nThe “simbolism” is fairly cryptic. The caption dates it around February 1950, so that might make it even harder to make sense of, as we’re talking about fairly early days when it comes to the final H-bomb design, but I’m not sure how reliable I find that dating. (The H-bomb debate was in late 1949-early 1950, though the caption was obviously written at a much later time.)\n\nLooking for some insight into the technical discussions that were happening at this time, I took a gander at Anne Fitzpatrick’s quite detailed thesis on the early history of the H-bomb, “Igniting the Light Elements: The Los ALamos Thermonuclear Weapons Project, 1942-1952,” (Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University, 1999), which was issued as LA-13577-T. Fitzpatrick’s work is notable as one of the few H-bomb histories that have been written by a non-participant but also with access to classified information. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Carving of Reality: Essays by the LessWrong Community vol. III by LessWrong | Goodreads (https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/180340958-the-carving-of-reality)\nciteturn546search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; \n\n# The Carving of Reality: Essays by the LessWrong Community vol. III\n\n### \n\nLessWrong\n\n4.50 6 ratings0 reviews\n\nWant to Read\n\nBuy on Amazon\n\nRate this book\n\n“Whenever there’s a cutting-edge new idea making the rounds, Eliezer was writing about it 5-10 years ago. A deep dive into LessWrong will make you smarter.” — Tim Urban, Author of \"Wait But Why\" Featuring essays by Scott Alexander, Zvi Mowshowitz, and over 30 more LessWrong writers. Each year, the LessWrong community votes on which posts were most valuable. We've compiled the winners of the third Annual Review into an anthology of four books. The essays explore questions like \"When is AGI likely to transform the world?\", \"What are the limits of Bayesian reasoning?\", and \"Why exactly is civilization so dysfunctional?\" The essays take a variety of forms, from thought experiments to literature reviews, as well as personal stories, microeconomic arguments, mathematical explanations, book reviews, interviews, research advice, philosophical musings, published papers, forecasts of the future, survey data, disagreements-with-Robin-Hanson, cartoons and more. Each essay is accompanied with unique piece of artwork, designed using machine learning software that created each image using the corresponding essay title and a diagram of the Mississippi River. “Essays from LessWrong have been one of my favorite sources of wisdom.” —Vitalik Buterin, Co-founder of Ethereum The essays were originally published on LessWrong in 2020, and for the first time are available in print form. “The rationality community is one of the brightest lights in the modern intellectual firmament.” —Bryan Caplan, Professor of Economics, George Mason University\n\nGenresPhilosophy\n\n1000 pages, Perfect Paperback\n\nPublished January 1, 2023\n\nBook details & editions\n\n### About the author\n\n#### LessWrong\n\n3 books 10 followers\n\nFollow\n\nFollow\n\nLessWrong is an online forum and community dedicated to improving human reasoning and decision-making. We seek to hold true beliefs and to be effective at accomplishing our goals. Each day, we aim to be less wrong about the world than the day before.\n\n\n## Ratings & Reviews\n\nWhat do you think?\n\nRate this book\n\nWrite a Review\n\n\n### Community Reviews\n\n4.50\n\n6 ratings